WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.030
It's twelve-oh-one on New Year's Day. The fireworks are over,

2
00:00:04.030 --> 00:00:07.408
and everybody wants a ride home. At the exact same time.

3
00:00:07.688 --> 00:00:09.389
You open the app, and... whoa.

4
00:00:10.339 --> 00:00:13.301
That's surge pricing, a type of dynamic pricing,

5
00:00:13.301 --> 00:00:16.326
where the price changes with demand in real time.

6
00:00:17.276 --> 00:00:20.324
Here's the economics. Thousands of riders want cars

7
00:00:20.324 --> 00:00:23.253
at once, so the demand curve shifts to the right.

8
00:00:23.533 --> 00:00:26.113
If fares stayed the same, there'd be way more

9
00:00:26.113 --> 00:00:28.980
riders than drivers, and long waits for everybody.

10
00:00:29.930 --> 00:00:31.539
Higher fares do two things.

11
00:00:31.819 --> 00:00:34.642
They pull more drivers onto the road, and they ration

12
00:00:34.642 --> 00:00:37.306
rides to the riders who value them most right now.

13
00:00:38.256 --> 00:00:42.484
Critics say it's price gouging, charging people more when they have no choice.

14
00:00:42.764 --> 00:00:46.704
Supporters say it gets more cars on the road, exactly when you need them.

15
00:00:47.654 --> 00:00:49.646
Your turn. Is surge pricing fair?

16
00:00:49.926 --> 00:00:52.358
Pick a side, and defend it with economics.
